This is a standard systems programming and security testing topic — simulating error signals to observe how a system responds. Here's a practical overview:

## Sending POSIX Signals (C)

```c
#include <signal.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int main() {
    pid_t target_pid = getpid(); // your own process for testing

    // Simulate common error signals
    kill(target_pid, SIGSEGV);  // segmentation fault
    kill(target_pid, SIGFPE);   // floating-point exception
    kill(target_pid, SIGABRT);  // abort
    kill(target_pid, SIGBUS);   // bus error
    kill(target_pid, SIGILL);   // illegal instruction
}
```

## Observing How a System Handles Signals

Register custom handlers to see what the default behavior is, then compare:

```c
#include <signal.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <unistd.h>

void handler(int sig, siginfo_t *info, void *context) {
    printf("Caught signal %d from PID %d\n", sig, info->si_pid);
    _exit(1);
}

int main() {
    struct sigaction sa;
    memset(&sa, 0, sizeof(sa));
    sa.sa_sigaction = handler;
    sa.sa_flags = SA_SIGINFO;

    sigaction(SIGSEGV, &sa, NULL);
    sigaction(SIGFPE, &sa, NULL);
    sigaction(SIGBUS, &sa, NULL);
    sigaction(SIGILL, &sa, NULL);

    // Trigger a real SIGSEGV
    int *p = NULL;
    *p = 42;

    return 0;
}
```

## Using `raise()` and `abort()` from Within a Process

```c
raise(SIGSEGV);  // send signal to yourself
abort();         // sends SIGABRT
```

## From the Shell

```bash
# Send signals to a running process
kill -SEGV <pid>
kill -FPE <pid>
kill -ABRT <pid>

# Watch what security mechanisms respond
dmesg | tail          # kernel log (crash reports, ASLR, stack protector)
journalctl -f         # systemd journal on Linux
log stream --level error  # macOS unified log
```

## Fault Injection for Security Testing

To systematically test how security measures respond:

| Technique | What it tests |
|---|---|
| NULL pointer dereference | ASLR, memory protection, crash reporting |
| Stack buffer overflow | Stack canaries, NX bit, ASLR |
| `raise(SIGSEGV)` | Core dump policy, crash handlers |
| `fork()` + signal in child | Process isolation, sandboxing |
| `ptrace` on self | Anti-debugging protections |
| `/proc/self/maps` inspection | ASLR randomization quality |

## Python (Quick Prototyping)

```python
import os
import signal

os.kill(os.getpid(), signal.SIGSEGV)
```

## Key Security Measures You Can Observe This Way

- **ASLR** — check `/proc/self/maps` across runs to see address randomization
- **Stack canaries** — overflow a buffer and watch for `__stack_chk_fail`
- **Core dumps** — check `ulimit -c` and `/proc/sys/kernel/core_pattern`
- **seccomp / sandbox** — certain signals or syscalls will be blocked
- **crash reporters** — macOS CrashReporter, Linux apport/systemd-coredump

Run these against **your own test processes** on systems you control. This is standard practice in penetration testing, fuzzing, and hardening audits.
